{
 "id": "2026-11-14-a",
 "title": "Why is gold gold? Blame Einstein ✨",
 "description": "Almost every metal is grey, so why is gold yellow? Gold's nucleus has 79 protons, so its inner electrons move at over half the speed of light. Einstein's relativity makes them heavier and pulls their orbits in, which tunes gold to absorb blue light. Take blue out of white light and what bounces back looks yellow. Without relativity, gold would look silvery, like silver.\n\nSources: Wikipedia, Relativistic quantum chemistry; Fourmilab, What gives gold that mellow glow?; Scientific American, Mercury, gold and Einstein's relativity.",
 "tags": [
